A binary matrix with `n` rows and `m` columns initially contains only zeros. Rows and columns are numbered from `1`. You must process `q` queries in order. Each query is a line `t c i` with one of four forms:

- `1 R i`: flip every cell of row `i` (each `0` becomes `1` and each `1` becomes `0`);
- `1 C i`: flip every cell of column `i`;
- `2 R i`: report the number of zeros currently in row `i`;
- `2 C i`: report the number of zeros currently in column `i`.

For every query of type `2`, print its answer on its own line, in the order the queries appear. If there are no queries of type `2`, print nothing.

